    "summary": "Alligator jam and Good Lovin' marathon drive a high‑energy Capitol set",
    "blurb": "The concert erupts with blistering Alligator and an extended Good Lovin′ > Main Ten > Good Lovin′ sequence that showcases Jerry’s sharp guitar work. Pigpen adds a fiery King Bee harp jam while the Rhythm Devils lock in a relentless groove. The performance is a quintessential 1970 Dead high‑water mark despite gritty audience tape quality.",
    "review": "Port Chester’s Capitol Theater on November 6, 1970 delivered one of the era’s most electrifying shows. The first set wound through acoustic staples – a gentle “Don’t Ease Me In,” a plaintive “Deep Elem Blues,” and an early “Uncle John’s Band” – before the band launched into a second‑set that never let up. Highlights include a snarling “Alligator” that finds Jerry ripping blistering blues riffs, a monumental Good Lovin′ > Main Ten > Good Lovin′ sandwich that feels almost ceremonial, and a seamless segue from Caution (Do Not Stop On Tracks) into Turn On Your Lovelight. Pigpen’s harmonica shines on an extended “King Bee” jam, and the dual drummers (Mickey Hart & Bill Kreutzmann) drive the jams with a thick, propulsive Rhythm Devils tone. While all four audience recordings suffer from the typical 1970 tape hiss and occasional drop‑outs, the Warner audience source captures enough of the set to let listeners experience the “radioactive” flow that has made this show legendary among Deadheads.",
